WEBVTT

00:00:03.072 --> 00:00:07.222
The third conversation is about rhythm: how do we work?

00:00:07.272 --> 00:00:10.642
Most teams don’t have a rhythm. They have a calendar.

00:00:10.692 --> 00:00:15.982
Meetings were added over time, each for a good reason, and nobody ever took one away.

00:00:16.032 --> 00:00:22.102
The result: lots of meetings, and still the feeling that the important conversations never happen.

00:00:22.152 --> 00:00:23.822
A good rhythm is simple.

00:00:23.872 --> 00:00:28.882
It has three moments: when you plan, when you make, and when you review.

00:00:28.932 --> 00:00:31.842
Planning is where you decide what matters most.

00:00:31.892 --> 00:00:35.842
Making is the time to actually do the work, ideally protected.

00:00:35.892 --> 00:00:39.462
And reviewing is where you look at what you did, and learn from it.

00:00:39.512 --> 00:00:45.362
Signs your team needs this conversation: the calendar is full but priorities are unclear.

00:00:45.412 --> 00:00:48.402
People say “we should have talked about this earlier.”

00:00:48.452 --> 00:00:50.682
Deep work happens in the evenings.

00:00:50.732 --> 00:00:55.082
Or nobody can remember the last time the team looked back at how things went.

00:00:55.132 --> 00:01:01.202
The output on the canvas is short. When and how we plan. When and how we make.

00:01:01.252 --> 00:01:03.122
When and how we review.

00:01:03.172 --> 00:01:05.722
And a list of meetings and habits we’re dropping.

00:01:05.772 --> 00:01:09.422
That last list is often the most popular part of the whole course.

00:01:09.472 --> 00:01:13.022
People love dropping meetings. But be thoughtful.

00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:15.782
Every meeting was created to solve a problem.

00:01:15.832 --> 00:01:20.673
Before you drop it, ask: “What problem was this solving, and how will we solve it now?”

